# These findings are in the npm CLI's own bundled dependency tree
# (installed globally via `npm install -g npm@10` in the Dockerfile, to
# replace the base image's stale npm — see Dockerfile comments), not in
# anything under this project's package.json/node_modules that the running
# server ever imports.
#
# npm is only ever invoked here as a build/boot-time CLI tool (`npm ci`,
# `npm run build`, `npx prisma migrate deploy`, `npm run start`) — it never
# runs as a network-facing process, and none of its own vendored packages
# (brace-expansion, ip-address, picomatch, sigstore — used internally by
# npm for install-time glob matching, registry auth, and provenance
# verification) ever parse attacker-controlled input in this deployment.
#
# Re-evaluate whenever the Dockerfile's npm version is bumped: re-run the
# container-scan job and drop any CVE below that Trivy no longer reports.
